#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091893 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1316" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\n- $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\n- $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091894 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1317" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1318" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\n- $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\n- $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "d0f6fda4-4083-5541-8f0e-9a61f740ba8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9981" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcf11d6-572f-57e3-af69-334511c72244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315408} {:answer/id #uuid "2260fcd5-3374-554d-abee-7b008df8f628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315409} {:answer/id #uuid "e398e850-417f-580c-88b4-456922585e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315410} {:answer/id #uuid "7de7608d-fb39-5c91-8866-00bc20a344fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315411} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f6e3b6-2354-58ad-bb9c-fb06da5e613e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e398e850-417f-580c-88b4-456922585e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315410} :answer-field/id #uuid "c81186fb-2a3d-5ae5-865f-8b1dc7654013" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1492386-6d0c-51d6-9c6a-e6f64d3cb61e" :question/math-academy-id "q-111385" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "771d660b-ae72-53ff-aa82-80c0a3243a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315415} {:answer/id #uuid "712e9ae8-b93b-533d-9fba-c670a1ce55fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315416} {:answer/id #uuid "8c79a1f6-1faa-5d64-8906-f514e2654892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315417} {:answer/id #uuid "4522e59e-0963-5636-a5b9-67b82da1f4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315418} {:answer/id #uuid "c62eec66-2f1f-574f-a9ef-a8145c8d3d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4522e59e-0963-5636-a5b9-67b82da1f4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315418} :answer-field/id #uuid "a814c045-d35a-5bdb-aa7a-ae5c09ccd3d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc10624a-1d97-52d8-b614-80cd282f5aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-111390" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9b97dfe-e863-5067-bd1f-9fe80a7da4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089431} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe063b-3ac8-5bae-886e-2ef8e28207c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089432} {:answer/id #uuid "16f477bf-e52c-5659-9a0d-6c656f1a72d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089433} {:answer/id #uuid "1aea45fa-1601-5ba3-9238-4fec5c076a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089434} {:answer/id #uuid "42142bd5-3fdf-567d-87bd-f25ee43bf67f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aea45fa-1601-5ba3-9238-4fec5c076a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089434} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc0adf4a-ebc6-5645-94d9-fd6ba43dae74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2c512bf-41d1-55e1-a589-f849ec8fac50" :question/math-academy-id "q-141203" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $({x}^{2}+4x)-(-5{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $(2-{x}^{3})÷({x}^{2})$\n- $(2x-{x}^{3})\\cdot ({x}^{2}-x+1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b59fdc75-fd8c-5168-a0c6-2aa8dae53efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315422} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc11679-aabd-5a6b-81fa-9bc49d982f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315423} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3c881e-b7a4-594b-aa34-899a8c060404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315424} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a4e71d-cde9-5e2d-b051-5e10b92f54b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315425} {:answer/id #uuid "63824682-6cd1-5d8e-b03d-af409b2c83d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f3c881e-b7a4-594b-aa34-899a8c060404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315424} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d48d013-a62b-561f-be1d-f3b4767c0f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43473837-68b6-5be4-987f-f091e37ac03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-141234" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n- $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f9b004-85c4-5f0d-9409-c6c087b84e0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315429} {:answer/id #uuid "3c35eb6b-9317-58c6-a1a2-d041b088d934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315430} {:answer/id #uuid "6265302e-71ae-51ef-a8a3-8bd45bb7403a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315431} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ad1e53-2956-5abb-88b0-13bfdbad64c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315432} {:answer/id #uuid "baec33aa-250a-52ad-a175-1ef23d7ad061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baec33aa-250a-52ad-a175-1ef23d7ad061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315433} :answer-field/id #uuid "67771a47-1176-5217-a18f-dbb5da86a9c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0455dfc-71d9-5697-8db9-62e19eeb0c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-141236" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$\n- $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{6}-2}{2x} & =\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{2x}-\\frac{2}{2x} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e16708c3-dece-5d8f-b9a9-d44aa1ae1d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315436} {:answer/id #uuid "777ad2ff-bff1-5c4f-ac5b-9608e6509b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315437} {:answer/id #uuid "042d5b69-3606-5780-b44c-539f0ee4a926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315438} {:answer/id #uuid "0693c42d-6d57-5b8d-8a90-c9dc6e2febc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315439} {:answer/id #uuid "b39c808d-a60c-5dda-9e0b-aab6b0634696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e16708c3-dece-5d8f-b9a9-d44aa1ae1d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315436} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0fbd1d9-1ff7-5fc6-825d-2de23b2c5361" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28e6d8df-ffe4-5059-9c8f-21069da9bee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141240" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$\n- $({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x({x}^{2}-4x)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}-4x)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7137a5-b877-553e-944f-d7526dbb3abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315443} {:answer/id #uuid "0f54b6a4-085c-5f85-866e-c1ea1e60bb87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315444} {:answer/id #uuid "eda44ca7-4f9c-5583-ae36-2030ee31bde4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315445} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc056f9-1fea-57f8-89ee-1d238636584d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315446} {:answer/id #uuid "83ab3ba6-3a6d-51e4-9109-32ac534d11c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f54b6a4-085c-5f85-866e-c1ea1e60bb87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315444} :answer-field/id #uuid "120e9f86-ba52-5322-aa73-b0b46dbc2ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bf388ce-2466-5600-a83d-d8315936fc03" :question/math-academy-id "q-141245" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(3{x}^{3}-2x)÷({x}^{3})$\n- $(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$\n- $(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32de8b38-a6ee-589f-9b5b-a7df66dd7cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315450} {:answer/id #uuid "f56788e2-2b21-5a3e-985b-9315b1fa51cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315451} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c1fb7e-4e79-5a9a-8b4a-f4a196caeafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315452} {:answer/id #uuid "41f4c6fe-936a-5a2b-847b-6b9dd71a79df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315453} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc511a9-04d4-5fe5-acd4-d35f3aa3efb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41f4c6fe-936a-5a2b-847b-6b9dd71a79df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315453} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb9ead80-670a-5921-90f8-2f877fee227b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3b48f20-e3af-5da4-b384-927a78607a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141250" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{2}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{2}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4+2}={x}^{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d456c80b-092e-5927-a47e-2a20cb348114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d456c80b-092e-5927-a47e-2a20cb348114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315457} :answer-field/id #uuid "428a0aa6-bef8-5caa-90f8-4090efd9f261" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f88ab8dd-7297-5a21-afd7-67cb8387d601" :question/math-academy-id "q-269884" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $11$ and $9$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $11,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{11}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{11}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{11}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{11+9}={x}^{20}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f4fc140-9ba6-5e23-b016-4f482624ecd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f4fc140-9ba6-5e23-b016-4f482624ecd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315460} :answer-field/id #uuid "03db6f4a-d725-586c-acfe-f5930688d0b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c76e7c60-ef17-596f-9e63-bcccdd6b1b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-269922" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $15$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=5{x}^{15}$ and $Q=5{x}^{15}-12.$ Then $P-Q=12,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd5f116-471f-524d-b2de-8da1e00e3c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfd5f116-471f-524d-b2de-8da1e00e3c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315463} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f718bb-bca5-5cfc-b8b8-9f64e0b317bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2e01581-c7c6-5b76-a600-2bf9f2103582" :question/math-academy-id "q-277896" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $6$ and $9$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $6,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{6}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{6}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{6+9}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6919a49e-e9a8-5a0f-9b22-adce85177cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315466} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b77b80-4cde-5a46-a4d4-b73c8cd6179d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6919a49e-e9a8-5a0f-9b22-adce85177cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315466} :answer-field/id #uuid "53c3f053-23d4-5142-9312-2dd493d775a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a270835-05f5-5e7f-936c-eb84b2cf80ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-277901" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $14$ and $8$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $14,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{14}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{8}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{14}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{14}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{14+8}={x}^{22}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $22.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ee5e5e7-98da-51da-95d5-5ed6d742e102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ee5e5e7-98da-51da-95d5-5ed6d742e102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315470} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a2ff56-d924-5308-a3d9-d68a2285860a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62208363-aadd-5a8b-bb77-eb9afb9030cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-277910" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=4{x}^{3}$ and $Q=4{x}^{3}-10.$ Then $P-Q=10,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e194b1f7-0215-5106-b160-7d4a59901293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315473} {:answer/id #uuid "6e64c7fa-33d6-58d1-8455-dca32c0d1cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315474} {:answer/id #uuid "63a4957a-3ce2-53a3-898a-918e10d10675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315475} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1ec2d2-7faa-5f7b-a057-55605f11c70d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315476} {:answer/id #uuid "10f920d3-2af7-53ff-a1e7-c9b869bd5278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a4957a-3ce2-53a3-898a-918e10d10675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315475} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b35059f-46f0-5f2d-8c00-39284e3240c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c7b2eef-6b92-58ae-a674-05e715b64388" :question/math-academy-id "q-46423" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$, what is the highest possible degree of $P+Q?$" :question/worked-solution "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $7.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{7}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{7}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf3c1a6-4fae-5432-9829-fb44f6d9e39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315480} {:answer/id #uuid "2b90353f-3c9c-5b3a-9e76-4cb45b1ab368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315481} {:answer/id #uuid "2db9db40-0c3e-5ae6-a489-4f3de1b2e834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315482} {:answer/id #uuid "6940806e-66ff-5f4b-b04c-3e3a77b03078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315483} {:answer/id #uuid "79e50c9b-54ab-5ca8-9942-bd8f19840749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b90353f-3c9c-5b3a-9e76-4cb45b1ab368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315481} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7461fdc-7ca0-5fd9-b3ab-3307f4c2d618" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84919779-23e7-5cc8-bcb5-832dfb1dc640" :question/math-academy-id "q-46424" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $10$ and $9$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $10,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{10}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{10}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{10}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{10+9}={x}^{19}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $19.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c33e62-0310-5ef0-b38b-39405ffa1570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315487} {:answer/id #uuid "34d5f8c4-8993-5bfd-8fbe-8c403d97d9d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315488} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3aeda1-5cc4-5eeb-afc4-6141bb54c775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315489} {:answer/id #uuid "12afee57-81d3-5ee3-a337-3f2ac3b1fe94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315490} {:answer/id #uuid "df419dcd-2f90-54e5-bd2d-2a97d54f3d7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c3aeda1-5cc4-5eeb-afc4-6141bb54c775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315489} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df65011-e755-50e0-b33a-445857115678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a38a30de-6cf8-595a-b6f6-e4a70684c026" :question/math-academy-id "q-46453" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $7$, respectively, what is the degree of $P-Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{2}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $7,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{7}.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{7}.$ No ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c24c3a29-99e1-5f8f-8b23-2e8474c5ac3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315494} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7584da-fd70-5df9-9319-69a82bb30798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315495} {:answer/id #uuid "552b463b-a7ca-5bc0-8103-1328a292e613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315496} {:answer/id #uuid "3c26f15b-6217-5432-9c72-cbde32464237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315497} {:answer/id #uuid "06547216-b45e-569d-8be2-32afdde2a745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c24c3a29-99e1-5f8f-8b23-2e8474c5ac3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315494} :answer-field/id #uuid "a53e87d7-6bec-53b1-add3-0dac17b9f9f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a307f126-3ea1-5bb3-93e8-d7d2229faeee" :question/math-academy-id "q-46456" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$ and $9$, respectively, what is the degree of $P-Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{5}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{9}.$ No ${x}^{9}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d11ab003-2440-5e69-9cf5-88cfaddcad5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090432} {:answer/id #uuid "36ae66df-0ea6-5421-8b72-67a89f84f5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090433} {:answer/id #uuid "4496fb53-4831-5dc2-978e-02266897190a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090434} {:answer/id #uuid "46f5c046-8295-5986-8048-2915d9ba8ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090435} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4791ba-530d-5bec-8525-ba6572add1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d11ab003-2440-5e69-9cf5-88cfaddcad5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090432} :answer-field/id #uuid "6439cbca-2e75-581a-916c-bd48c79e58fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f528577-792d-5dde-937b-39993322cd56" :question/math-academy-id "q-46457" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $6,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{6},$ respectively. In $P+Q,$ the leading term still contains an ${x}^{6}.$ There is no ${x}^{6}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{6}$ term in $Q.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $6.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{6}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7ba49c-4a26-54e2-bd75-42ad05f3b4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294763} {:answer/id #uuid "04abffd8-7053-53e6-b817-84082d046c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294764} {:answer/id #uuid "5a36e3d7-210d-5b4f-a85a-040387b79fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294765} {:answer/id #uuid "9075ff78-741a-5729-812d-2a80fd97eb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294766} {:answer/id #uuid "430bfad4-077d-5435-8e5c-52a43682396e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9075ff78-741a-5729-812d-2a80fd97eb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294766} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a971d13-8c2e-5ca1-b66c-f3ad09971c24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c510ba2-7634-5ceb-8cb3-a5b35044f36d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47447" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dce5b35-f1df-585a-ba39-72729a85473d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315501} {:answer/id #uuid "eee7dea7-eb3f-591f-922d-c93a7b4293fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315502} {:answer/id #uuid "941528ec-baeb-5cf8-818d-f4748219ebdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315503} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0f3521-188e-534e-a080-ab5bbfff4d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315504} {:answer/id #uuid "641553a4-68f4-59a2-9675-7d126a1fdb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0f3521-188e-534e-a080-ab5bbfff4d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315504} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97a78ab-ed0a-5203-a2a4-f3508b6bad51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cff243a3-fd98-53b6-858c-867e5a03ee26" :question/math-academy-id "q-51690" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $3{x}^{3}-P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $3{x}^{3}+P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P=3{x}^{3}-1.$ Then $3{x}^{3}-P(x)=3{x}^{3}-(3{x}^{3}-1)=1,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P=-3{x}^{3}.$ Then $3{x}^{3}+P(x)=3{x}^{3}+(-3{x}^{3})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{3}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}={x}^{3+3}={x}^{6},$ meaning that $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $6.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}]}
